{
  "dom_id": "step-q119896",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/212a339585b98f156d68f2e4260c0acbb474c51782b6d72acf0b89fd5c1fe69e.png)\n\nGiven the vectors $a$ and $b$ shown above, which of the following could be the linear combination $2a-2b?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall that\n\n$$\n2a-2b=2a+(-2b).\n$$\n\n- The vector $2a$ has the same direction as $a$ but twice the magnitude.\n\n- The vector $-2b$ has the direction that is opposite to $b$ but twice the magnitude.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/c1cc845ddfe16f12169625e5f8404d3d92558b885ffde8c3ab02cdb2c096f699.png)\n\nNow, we can draw $-2b$ starting from the head of $2a.$ The resultant \n\n$$\n2a-2b\n$$\n\ngoes from the tail of $2a$ to the head of $-2b.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/5d22db7e37ff1c5b55f1ec595da919f987a6c1fea4247cf5a075f27c198ba545.png)",
